KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ differing element types: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ second argument of g is not const: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.prim.req.general]/4, [temp.variadic]/5: in `requires(A &&...a)'
the pack declares one parameter per element of A, the k-th of type A_k &&.
With A = {const int &, int &} the front end gives the parameters one and the
same type ("type mismatch on static_cast: operand type 'const signed int',
cast type 'signed int &'", suppressed), so the requirement is unsatisfied;
with equal element types it works.  Root cause of the libc++ (LLVM 18)
drops of __format's __get_property and __estimated_width in every C++20
program including <vector> or <span> (cpp20_vector_basic_libcxx and 11
other passing libcxx tests): libc++'s std::invocable is `requires(_Fn
&&__fn, _Args &&...__args)', and ranges::upper_bound's
indirect_strict_weak_order needs invocable<less &, const uint32_t &,
uint32_t &>.  `std::ranges::upper_bound(array, v)' is then a conversion
error.  Replacing std::invocable by is_invocable_v in a header overlay
removes both drops, and also libc++'s std::function drops at
__functional/function.h:272 and 350 (the remaining one, 394
~__value_func, is cpp11_static_member_of_recursive_partial_specialization's
aligned_storage).  Pre-existing (bin53).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept
this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
